1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,625
The files you need are not on this machine.

2
00:00:02,645 --> 00:00:06,801
They are on another server, and you need them right here, as if they were local.

3
00:00:07,271 --> 00:00:11,817
In this video: mount and unmount network file systems using N F S.

4
00:00:16,337 --> 00:00:18,920
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:19,390 --> 00:00:23,060
Mount and unmount network file systems using NFS.

6
00:00:23,530 --> 00:00:26,817
We'll look at what a server shares, mount that share on a folder,

7
00:00:26,837 --> 00:00:28,801
read a file through it, and unmount it.

8
00:00:37,321 --> 00:00:42,313
N F S, the Network File System, lets one machine share a folder over the network.

9
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:47,264
The machine that shares is the server. The machine that mounts it is the client.

10
00:00:51,734 --> 00:00:56,917
On the client, a network share is mounted just like a disk, with the mount command.

11
00:00:56,937 --> 00:00:58,861
But the source is not a device.

12
00:00:58,881 --> 00:01:02,999
It is the server name, a colon, and the path of the shared folder.

13
00:01:08,469 --> 00:01:11,265
In real life, the server is another machine.

14
00:01:11,285 --> 00:01:15,425
In our lab, to keep it simple, the same machine plays both roles,

15
00:01:15,445 --> 00:01:17,025
and we call it localhost.

16
00:01:17,045 --> 00:01:21,057
Before the demo, silently, the lab installs the N F S packages,

17
00:01:21,077 --> 00:01:24,577
creates the shared folder, and starts the N F S server.

18
00:01:24,597 --> 00:01:28,097
On the exam, the server will already be set up for you.

19
00:01:37,617 --> 00:01:40,100
First we look at what the server shares.

20
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:43,668
Showmount dash e, and the server name, lists its exports.

21
00:01:43,688 --> 00:01:47,986
Here the server localhost shares one folder: slash S R V slash share.

22
00:01:57,506 --> 00:02:01,326
We need an empty folder to mount on, so we make one with mkdir.

23
00:02:04,796 --> 00:02:09,413
Now mount, with dash t N F S to say the type, then the server colon path,

24
00:02:09,433 --> 00:02:10,429
then our folder.

25
00:02:16,899 --> 00:02:19,341
D F with dash h T shows the result.

26
00:02:19,361 --> 00:02:24,477
The type is N F S four, and the source is the server and the shared path.

27
00:02:24,497 --> 00:02:27,994
The size is the size of the server disk, not ours.

28
00:02:37,514 --> 00:02:39,227
We use it like any folder.

29
00:02:39,247 --> 00:02:43,361
Ls shows hello dot txt, and cat prints what the server stored.

30
00:02:49,831 --> 00:02:54,254
Findmnt shows the details: the source, the type, and the options.

31
00:02:54,274 --> 00:02:59,859
Version four point two, and proto tcp. Do not worry about the other options today.

32
00:03:11,379 --> 00:03:15,818
To unmount, umount with the folder. As before: no N after the U.

33
00:03:21,288 --> 00:03:26,501
Findmnt prints nothing, so it is not mounted any more. And ls shows an empty folder:

34
00:03:26,521 --> 00:03:28,308
the files stay on the server.

35
00:03:35,828 --> 00:03:39,264
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

36
00:03:40,784 --> 00:03:44,264
We reboot. The mount is gone, because we mounted by hand.

37
00:03:44,284 --> 00:03:48,623
The server still lists its share. We mount again, and the file is back.

38
00:03:48,643 --> 00:03:51,324
Making the mount permanent is a later topic.

39
00:04:04,844 --> 00:04:09,891
Your turn. A server called localhost shares slash S R V slash exam.

40
00:04:09,911 --> 00:04:13,748
Mount it on slash M N T slash exam with type N F S,

41
00:04:13,768 --> 00:04:19,571
and read hello dot txt through the mount. Then unmount slash M N T slash old.

42
00:04:19,591 --> 00:04:20,781
Run the checker.

43
00:04:28,251 --> 00:04:30,002
Try before you open the solution.

44
00:04:35,522 --> 00:04:37,742
Next up: configure autofs.

45
00:04:38,212 --> 00:04:41,115
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
